{"step_index":70,"source":"MODEL","type":"PLANNER_RESPONSE","status":"DONE","created_at":"2026-09-13T07:03:08Z","thinking":"**Defining the Approach**\n\nI'm currently focused on defining the project's parameters. The goal is clear: a 2D stickman, drawn and then animated. My approach prioritizes mathematical smoothness, which makes Manim an ideal tool. We have Manim ready, and I'm figuring out how to best integrate it.\n\n\n**Implementing the Solution**\n\nNow, I'm diving into the implementation using Manim. I'll create a Python script to draw the stickman with math-based shapes (circle head, lines/arcs for the body, limbs). I'll start with the drawing animation, and then move on to animating the stickman. I'm leaning toward a waving motion combined with a little jump or dance, all driven by trigonometric functions to achieve that perfect mathematical smoothness with parametric equations.\n\n\n**Constructing the Geometry**\n\nI've been working on defining the precise geometry of the stickman. I'm focusing on lines and circles for a basic shape. Each limb will be a line segment and each joint is a point for the pivot. I'm setting this up to enable mathematical animation of angles later. This should set a strong foundation for the planned mathematical animation.\n\n\n**Refining the Animation**\n\nI've got the Manim code underway. I'm using `ValueTracker` for time-based animation. Head motion uses a sine wave for bounce, and the body follows. Arms and legs are being generated with angle calculations, also driven by sine and cosine functions. It's shaping up to be mathematically elegant, which is exactly what I was hoping for.\n\n\n**Developing Limb Movements**\n\nI'm now focusing on implementing the limb movements, specifically the arms and legs. I'm using `always_redraw` to keep these components updating live. The arms are being built with shoulder, elbow, and hand calculations driven by the angle calculations. The legs utilize a walking/dancing motion, which is controlled via sine functions to create a smooth, natural-looking stride. I'm taking care to keep the knees bending backwards naturally.\n\n\n**Optimizing the Implementation**\n\nI'm refining the Manim code for the stickman animation, specifically focusing on the leg movements. To make it perform with the best animations, I'm working with the `always_redraw` functionality. To get the legs animated, I'm using trigonometric functions to define the foot position, based on knee position and a calculated angle. I've realized that using `always_redraw` with a static create animation might be tricky. It may be better to start the `ValueTracker` at `t=0` and keep the code the same.\n\n\n**Finalizing the Animation**\n\nI've got the animation method refined and simplified, now. I'm moving toward an implementation using forward kinematics and leveraging trigonometric functions. I will define a ValueTracker for the time variable `t`. The head, body, arms and legs will all depend on `t` for calculations. I will then play the create animation for the components and use `t.animate.set_value(TAU * 2)` to animate the stickman. The head will bob and the right arm will wave based on sine functions.\n\n\n**Animating the Legs**\n\nI'm now fully immersed in making the stickman dance. I'm focusing on the `hip` position derived from the `body`. I'm using `t` as a `ValueTracker` for time to generate a smooth trigonometric dance with a robust script and I've started the implementation. I'm building it with mathematical precision, specifically sine functions to get the hip position correct.\n\n```python\n    from manim import *\n    import numpy as np\n\n    class StickmanDance(Scene):\n        def construct(self):\n            t = ValueTracker(0)\n\n            # Static configuration so t=0 looks like a normal standing pose\n            def get_head():\n                # Bobbing up and down\n                y_offset = 0.2 * np.sin(2 * t.get_value())\n                return Circle(radius=0.5, color=YELLOW, stroke_width=6).shift(UP * (1.5 + y_offset))\n\n            def get_body(head):\n                return Line(head.get_bottom(), head.get_bottom() + DOWN * 2, color=WHITE, stroke_width=6)\n\n            def get_limb(start_point, angle1, length1, angle2, length2, color=WHITE):\n                joint = start_point + np.array([np.cos(angle1), np.sin(angle1), 0]) * length1\n                end = joint + np.array([np.cos(angle2), np.sin(angle2), 0]) * length2\n```\n\n\n**Refining the Geometry**\n\nI'm now integrating the components. I've designed `get_head` to bob, and the body's position follows accordingly. The right arm waves and the left arm swings smoothly, all updated with `always_redraw` and trig functions. I am building the legs with mathematical precision, specifically sine functions to get the hip position correct.\n\n\n**Integrating the Dance**\n\nI've integrated the components to complete the dancing stickman. The `get_head` bobs, and the body position reacts, as intended. I've designed smooth right arm waves and a swinging left arm using `always_redraw` driven by trig functions. The legs have been integrated using sine functions. I'm focusing now on precise hip positioning.\n\n\n**Executing the Script**\n\nI'm now running the Manim script and addressing rendering issues, specifically. The initial execution of the script is complete, and I'm currently assessing the output to refine the animation's smoothness. I'm focusing on ensuring the script executes without errors, and the output is precisely what I'm expecting.\n\n\n**Validating the Angles**\n\nI'm verifying the trigonometric logic for limb movements, making sure angles align with Manim's coordinate system. The right arm will move to the right, and the left arm to the left. I'm taking care of the floating, with the legs \"touching the floor.\"\n\nI'm confirming angles from the positive X axis.\n\n\n**Adding Visual Cues**\n\nI'm now integrating visual cues to the dance. I decided to include a simple title: \"Math in Motion: Smooth Trigonometry\" to reinforce the project's focus. The dance itself will be enhanced with trigonometric functions to emphasize mathematical smoothness, as planned. I'm making sure to keep the code clear and the animation focused.\n\n\n**Refining the Aesthetics**\n\nI'm now focusing on refining the visual cues and fine-tuning the animation's look. The stickman's movements are in place, so I'm now polishing the visual style and making it as pleasing as possible. I want the dance to look good, and will focus on keeping it mathematically smooth.\n\n\n**Enhancing the Integration**\n\nThe script is now fully integrated, and I am reviewing the output. I've designed the code to make sure that the character's geometry holds up to the trigonometry that will be used. The animation is shaping up, and I can start testing the math to make sure the stickman does not appear to be floating.\n\n\n\n**Validating the Code**\n\nI'm now diving deep into the code, rigorously testing and validating. I'm focusing on ensuring all limb calculations are mathematically sound and produce the expected motions. I'm verifying the trigonometric functions and I am running the code to validate the positions in the code block.\n\n```python\n        left_arm = always_redraw(lambda: get_limb(\n            start_point=body.get_end(),\n            angle1=-PI/4 + 0.3 * np.sin(2 * t.get_value()), \n            length1=1.4,\n            angle2=-PI/4 + 0.3 * np.sin(2 * t.get_value()) - PI/4,\n            length2=1.4,\n            color=GREEN\n        ))\n```\nI'm debugging and testing `left_arm` and `right_arm` to make sure the trig functions work!\n\n\n**Validating the Logic**\n\nI'm now meticulously reviewing and validating the code's logic and syntax. I've re-examined the use of `ValueTracker`, `always_redraw`, and `get_limb` and made a good start on validating the trig, and found a good starting pose. The key is in the right runtime, and I'll debug and run!\n\n\n","tool_calls":[{"name":"write_to_file","args":{"CodeContent":"from manim import *\nimport numpy as np\n\nclass StickmanDance(Scene):\n    def construct(self):\n        # We use a ValueTracker to drive time 't'. \n        # All limbs will be mathematically driven by sine and cosine waves based on this 't'.\n        t = ValueTracker(0)\n\n        # Title\n        title = Text(\"Math in Motion: Smooth Trigonometry\", font_size=36, color=WHITE)\n        title.to_edge(UP)\n        self.play(Write(title))\n\n        # Static configuration so t=0 looks like a normal standing pose\n        def get_head():\n            # The body bobs up and down smoothly using sin(2t)\n            y_offset = 0.2 * np.sin(2 * t.get_value())\n            return Circle(radius=0.5, color=YELLOW, stroke_width=6).shift(UP * (1.2 + y_offset))\n\n        def get_body(head):\n            return Line(head.get_bottom(), head.get_bottom() + DOWN * 2, color=WHITE, stroke_width=6)\n\n        # Helper function to compute limb positions using forward kinematics (pure math!)\n        def get_limb(start_point, angle1, length1, angle2, length2, color=WHITE):\n            # Calculate the elbow/knee joint position using cosine and sine\n            joint = start_point + np.array([np.cos(angle1), np.sin(angle1), 0]) * length1\n            # Calculate the hand/foot position relative to the joint\n            end = joint + np.array([np.cos(angle2), np.sin(angle2), 0]) * length2\n            \n            return VGroup(\n                Line(start_point, joint, color=color, stroke_width=6),\n                Line(joint, end, color=color, stroke_width=6),\n                Dot(joint, color=color, radius=0.1) # Draw the joint!\n            )\n\n        # Register the dynamic parts using always_redraw\n        head = always_redraw(get_head)\n        body = always_redraw(lambda: get_body(head))\n        \n        # Right Arm (Waving enthusiastically - uses 3x time multiplier for fast wave)\n        right_arm = always_redraw(lambda: get_limb(\n            start_point=body.get_start() + DOWN * 0.3,\n            angle1=PI/8 + 0.4 * np.sin(3 * t.get_value()), \n            length1=1.2,\n            angle2=PI/3 + 0.8 * np.sin(3 * t.get_value() - PI/4),\n            length2=1.0,\n            color=BLUE\n        ))\n\n        # Left Arm (Swinging subtly)\n        left_arm = always_redraw(lambda: get_limb(\n            start_point=body.get_start() + DOWN * 0.3,\n            angle1=-PI - PI/6 + 0.3 * np.sin(2 * t.get_value()), \n            length1=1.2,\n            angle2=-PI/2 - PI/4 + 0.4 * np.sin(2 * t.get_value() - PI/4),\n            length2=1.0,\n            color=RED\n        ))\n\n        # Right Leg (Dancing/Stepping - mix of sine and cosine for circular foot path)\n        right_leg = always_redraw(lambda: get_limb(\n            start_point=body.get_end(),\n            angle1=-PI/2 - PI/8 + 0.5 * np.sin(2 * t.get_value()), \n            length1=1.4,\n            angle2=-PI/2 - PI/8 + 0.5 * np.sin(2 * t.get_value()) - PI/4 + 0.4 * np.cos(2*t.get_value()),\n            length2=1.4,\n            color=GREEN\n        ))\n\n        # Left Leg (Planted but swaying slightly to keep balance)\n        left_leg = always_redraw(lambda: get_limb(\n            start_point=body.get_end(),\n            angle1=-PI/2 + PI/8 - 0.2 * np.sin(2 * t.get_value()), \n            length1=1.4,\n            angle2=-PI/2 + PI/8 - 0.2 * np.sin(2 * t.get_value()) + PI/6,\n            length2=1.4,\n            color=ORANGE\n        ))\n\n        stickman = VGroup(left_arm, right_arm, left_leg, right_leg, body, head)\n        \n        # Add a floor\n        floor = Line(LEFT*6, RIGHT*6, color=GREY).shift(DOWN*3.5)\n        self.play(Create(floor))\n\n        # === STEP 1: Painting the stickman ===\n        # Because t=0, the always_redraw functions stay completely still while we draw them!\n        self.play(Create(head), run_time=1)\n        self.play(Create(body), run_time=0.5)\n        self.play(Create(left_arm), Create(right_arm), run_time=1.2)\n        self.play(Create(left_leg), Create(right_leg), run_time=1.2)\n        self.wait(0.5)\n\n        # Show a quick text explaining the math\n        math_text = Text(\"Limbs driven by Sine & Cosine functions\", font_size=24, color=GREY_B)\n        math_text.next_to(title, DOWN)\n        self.play(FadeIn(math_text, shift=UP))\n\n        # === STEP 2: Animate with perfect mathematical smoothness ===\n        # We increase 't' from 0 to 4π. The always_redraw functions automatically calculate \n        # the perfect sub-pixel position for every single frame using sine and cosine!\n        self.play(t.animate.set_value(4 * PI), run_time=8, rate_func=linear)\n        \n        # === STEP 3: Cleanup ===\n        self.wait(1)\n        self.play(FadeOut(stickman), FadeOut(floor), FadeOut(title), FadeOut(math_text))\n","Description":"2D stickman drawn piece-by-piece, then animated smoothly using sine and cosine wave math tracking.","Overwrite":true,"TargetFile":"C:\\Users\\renu5\\.gemini\\antigravity\\scratch\\manim-projects\\stickman.py","toolAction":"Creating stickman script","toolSummary":"Stickman animation script"}}]}
