{"step_index":79,"source":"MODEL","type":"PLANNER_RESPONSE","status":"DONE","created_at":"2026-09-13T07:13:10Z","content":"The animation is done! I've used pure trigonometric mathematics (sine and cosine waves) to draw and smoothly animate a stickman dancing and waving.\n\n---\n\n## 🕺 Smooth Mathematical Stickman \n\n📁 **Your video file (Double-click to play!):**\n\n[StickmanDance.mp4](file:///C:/Users/renu5/.gemini/antigravity/brain/6f71b575-d73e-4413-b861-a66d8f356c8c/StickmanDance.mp4)\n\n### How I Used Math to Make It Smooth:\n1. **The Painting Phase:** The animation starts by constructing the stickman line by line, exactly as you requested (a \"2D painting\").\n2. **The \"Time\" Variable:** I created a mathematical `t` variable (time) that constantly increases.\n3. **Trigonometric Joints (Inverse/Forward Kinematics):**\n   * The **body** bobs up and down using a sine wave: `y = 0.2 * sin(2*t)`.\n   * The **arms and legs** are calculated using circular math: `X = length * cos(angle)` and `Y = length * sin(angle)`.\n   * The **waving hand** moves super fast by multiplying the sine wave time: `sin(3*t)`.\n   * The **legs** sway side-to-side out of sync with each other to simulate a smooth dancing motion.\n\nBecause Manim evaluates these mathematical functions for *every single frame* (30 times per second), the resulting animation has perfect, sub-pixel mathematical smoothness! Let me know if you want to make him do something else, like a backflip or change colors!"}
